{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "id": "fox-craftsperson-v2",
  "subject": "A small red fox craftsperson with a sea-green scarf, a blue sketchbook and a golden pencil.",
  "style": "Warm tactile storybook toy, rounded smooth forms, expressive head, clear material blocks and complete stylized 3D geometry.",
  "prompt": "Create an editable, controllable full-body stylized red fox craftsperson. Preserve the red-orange coat, cream muzzle/chest/tail tip, dark sock paws, oversized triangular ears, sea-green scarf, navy sketchbook, golden pencil and curled sweeping tail in every state. Author unseen surfaces consistently; this is an original toy-like 3D design, not a recovered scan. Idle should breathe and listen, making_something should sketch with repeated short pencil strokes, working should read the sketchbook with a slow gaze scan, and milestone_level_up should lift both tools and hop once. Keep the camera fixed and the whole body visible. Revision: keep the existing palette, face, proportions and camera. Close elbow and knee gaps with small smooth joints. For milestone_level_up, raise the shoulders and bend both forearms until paws and held tools are above shoulder height; include one gentle upward hop. Keep book facing the viewer at the peak. Further working-state refinement: visibly lift the book toward the face with the left arm, lower the gaze and scan the raised page. Keep the right pencil hand resting so reading and sketching are clearly different actions.",
  "authored_assumptions": [
    "Original primitive-based full 3D design; no source mesh or photographic reconstruction.",
    "Soft toy proportions and unseen back surfaces are authored.",
    "Each visible part is rigidly weighted to one articulated bone; this is a controllable toy rig, not deforming skin or physical simulation.",
    "Sketchbook pages are solid geometry; pencil motion conveys sketching without creating a dynamic ink texture.",
    "Keyframe rotations and translations are expressed in model rest axes (X right, Y rear, Z up). The builder converts these offsets through each bone rest basis before writing Blender pose transforms.",
    "Revision v2 uses visible toy elbow/knee joints and jointly authored shoulder/forearm/hand poses for a raised-tool celebration."
  ],
